window.CWC_V92_DATA={"version":"9.8.4","cases":[{"id":"pickup","title":"The Pickup That Became a Crisis","subtitle":"An adult confrontation exposes a staffing and safeguarding failure","tag":"Family conflict · Safeguarding","minutes":18,"variants":[{"id":"afternoon","label":"Afternoon pickup","start":"At 2:14 PM the director briefly uses the restroom. A qualified caregiver supervises six children when an angry parent enters the children's area.","overrides":{"0":{"question":"During an afternoon pickup, the only available teacher must protect six children while the director is out of the room. What action comes before collecting video or debating the allegation?","optionText":{"protect":"Move the children out of the confrontation while maintaining qualified supervision, and summon an available qualified colleague.","video":"Keep the children in place while a colleague retrieves the camera clip so the adult can see the accusation is unfounded.","escort":"Escort the parent into the office immediately, leaving the six children briefly unsupervised until the director returns.","debate":"Ask the child and parent to explain the conflict in front of the other children before separating anyone."}},"1":{"lead":"The parent has left after the afternoon confrontation. The camera captures the parent's behavior, six other children, and the teacher's response. The parent now demands a copy before the end of the day.","question":"How should you protect the original recording and the other children's confidentiality before answering that demand?"}}},{"id":"morning","label":"Morning drop-off","start":"During a crowded 8:10 AM arrival, the director steps into the restroom. One qualified caregiver remains with six children as a parent enters angrily.","overrides":{"0":{"question":"At morning drop-off, more families are approaching the doorway as an angry adult confronts a crying child. How do you stop the confrontation without creating a second supervision risk?","optionText":{"protect":"Direct incoming families away from the disturbance, protect the crying child, keep the six children continuously supervised, and request qualified backup.","video":"Pause the arrival line until staff can show video proving the adult is wrong, then resume check-in.","escort":"Leave the active arrival area to escort the adult to a private office without arranging qualified supervision for the children.","debate":"Bring the targeted child and adult together in the entryway to hear both accounts while families continue arriving."}},"1":{"lead":"The morning arrival camera also shows children and families entering behind the confrontation. A parent asks to inspect the recording immediately while a new arrival queue is forming.","question":"What is your responsibility to preserve evidence and protect the identities of children in the arrival footage?","optionText":{"preserve":"Secure the original footage and contemporaneous notes, route any viewing request through the authorized process, and maintain safe check-in.","show":"Replay the clip for the aggrieved parent at the entrance once everyone in the line promises confidentiality.","delete":"Write an incident summary and erase the footage before the morning rush ends to protect other children's privacy."}}}},{"id":"late","label":"Friday closing","start":"At 5:38 PM, just before closing, the director uses the restroom. One caregiver supervises six remaining children when a parent enters in anger.","overrides":{"0":{"question":"At Friday closing, one qualified teacher supervises the last six children. The confrontational adult refuses to leave. What do you do with no additional closing teacher immediately available?","optionText":{"protect":"Move the children away from the adult while maintaining direct qualified supervision, and request appropriate support or emergency help if needed.","video":"Use the final minutes before closing to locate video proof and argue the accusation with the adult.","escort":"Walk the adult outside yourself, leaving the children behind until the director finishes in the restroom.","debate":"Have the targeted child answer the adult's accusations so the dispute can be resolved before pickup ends."}},"1":{"lead":"Closing staff have discovered a recording that may be overwritten during weekend maintenance. The clip also contains identifiable children. The adult demands to see it before Monday.","question":"How should the director preserve a potentially time-sensitive recording without granting unauthorized access?","optionText":{"preserve":"Secure the original recording before any scheduled overwrite, document the timeline, and handle viewing requests through approved channels.","show":"Let the adult return for a private Friday evening viewing as long as they agree not to mention the other children.","delete":"Preserve a written staff description and let the weekend overwrite erase the clip once the director has watched it."}},"5":{"lead":"On Monday morning, the opening teacher calls out sick. Friday's closing incident exposed that the schedule has no reliable qualified backup for breaks or multiple interruptions.","question":"Before accepting Monday's arriving children, what staffing system must the director verify?"}}}],"stages":[{"title":"The adult enters","lead":"The parent shouts demeaning insults at a child, who begins crying. Other children witness the confrontation. The available employee has no immediate qualified backup.","question":"What should staff prioritize?","options":[{"id":"protect","text":"Separate the children from the adult, maintain direct supervision, and request qualified support while stopping the confrontation.","quality":2,"feedback":"Immediate protection and continuous supervision come first.","consequence":"The targeted child is moved to safety. The employee still needs another qualified adult to manage the remaining demands.","updates":{"safety":"Child protected","coverage":"Backup needed","family":"Parent access restricted"},"critical":false},{"id":"video","text":"Ask a colleague to retrieve the recording first so you can correct the parent's accusation with evidence before deciding on access restrictions.","quality":0,"feedback":"Proving the original accusation is not more urgent than protecting children.","consequence":"The confrontation continues while staff retrieve evidence; the targeted child remains exposed.","updates":{"safety":"Immediate danger unresolved","coverage":"Divided attention"},"critical":true},{"id":"escort","text":"Escort the adult to the front office to calm the conflict, even if doing so temporarily leaves the group without a confirmed supervisor.","quality":0,"feedback":"Removing the adult cannot justify abandoning necessary supervision.","consequence":"The parent leaves, but children were left without necessary oversight. Two safety problems now require attention.","updates":{"safety":"Supervision breached","coverage":"Unsupervised interval"},"critical":true},{"id":"debate","text":"Invite the parent and targeted child to explain their disagreement with staff present, allowing both sides to be heard before separating them.","quality":0,"feedback":"A child should not have to defend herself while an adult is intimidating her.","consequence":"The child becomes more distressed, and staff have delayed protection.","updates":{"safety":"Protection delayed","family":"Conflict escalated"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The video request","lead":"After the adult leaves, staff identify camera footage contradicting the accusation. The recording also shows other children.","question":"How should the director handle the recording and the parent's demand to see it?","options":[{"id":"preserve","text":"Secure the original footage and factual notes; send any viewing request through an authorized process.","quality":2,"feedback":"Evidence protection, accurate documentation, and confidentiality can operate together.","consequence":"The original recording is retained without exposing the identities or private activities of other children.","updates":{"evidence":"Original secured"},"critical":false},{"id":"show","text":"Offer a private viewing once the parent agrees not to discuss the other children who appear in the recording.","quality":0,"feedback":"Returning the threatening adult to the room exposes children to another confrontation and may disclose other children's information.","consequence":"The adult sees the footage but renews the argument in front of children.","updates":{"safety":"Re-exposure risk","evidence":"Privacy compromised"},"critical":true},{"id":"delete","text":"Keep a written summary and remove the video after staff have confirmed the accusation was false, reducing stored sensitive material.","quality":0,"feedback":"Destroying relevant evidence prevents later verification and can undermine protective review.","consequence":"The evidence cannot be independently assessed during later proceedings.","updates":{"evidence":"Evidence lost"},"critical":false}],"branchLead":{"protect":"The child is safe, but the parent disputes the accusation and demands to see the security recording.","video":"Staff eventually stop the argument, but the delay has increased the child's distress. The parent demands footage.","escort":"After the supervision lapse is addressed, the parent demands to view footage.","debate":"The argument ends only when another adult intervenes. The parent now demands to see the recording."}},{"title":"A new allegation","lead":"Another employee says she heard that the parent instructed her own child to mistreat the targeted child. The source is secondhand.","question":"How should this new information affect the director's response?","options":[{"id":"separate","text":"Record the exact source of the claim, protect the children, and assess reporting duties without declaring it proven.","quality":2,"feedback":"A report can be required without proof, but unverified claims must not be presented as established facts.","consequence":"The record separates observations, recording, and allegation. Staff implement an immediate protection plan.","updates":{"evidence":"Claims classified","safety":"Interim safeguards"},"critical":false},{"id":"declare","text":"Advise the families that coaching probably occurred because several employees have heard similar concerns about the parent.","quality":0,"feedback":"This converts uncertain information into a finding and can expose children to retaliation.","consequence":"The unsupported claim spreads and damages trust while the underlying risk is still unresolved.","updates":{"evidence":"Unsupported finding","family":"Conflict widened"},"critical":false},{"id":"ignore","text":"Ask staff to watch for another incident before documenting the concern, because the present account cannot be confirmed.","quality":0,"feedback":"Reasonable-suspicion reporting does not require video or proof; interim safety can be necessary.","consequence":"A potentially important safety concern remains unexamined.","updates":{"safety":"Ongoing concern overlooked"},"critical":false}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"Child welfare involvement","lead":"The family receives child care support connected to reunification services. Staff know some prior history but have not verified the alleged coaching.","question":"How should the director proceed?","options":[{"id":"coordinate","text":"Protect both children, verify information-sharing authority, coordinate as appropriate, and meet reporting duties.","quality":2,"feedback":"Child welfare involvement adds coordination duties, not permission to ignore present conduct or assume guilt from past history.","consequence":"The appropriate agency receives verified information, and the program maintains immediate safeguards.","updates":{"family":"Agency coordination","evidence":"Verified facts shared"},"critical":false},{"id":"history","text":"End the child's enrollment because the reunification history establishes the parent's unreliability, then inform the caseworker.","quality":0,"feedback":"History alone cannot establish what happened in the present incident.","consequence":"A biased assumption replaces a defensible risk assessment.","updates":{"evidence":"Historical bias"},"critical":false},{"id":"defer","text":"Request directions from the family's caseworker and postpone the facility's own safety decision until that agency responds.","quality":0,"feedback":"Immediate child protection belongs to the facility even while agencies coordinate.","consequence":"The program remains exposed while awaiting an external response.","updates":{"safety":"Protection deferred"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The enrollment decision","lead":"The targeted child's family asks how the facility will prevent further intimidation. The threatening adult may return, and peer mistreatment continues.","question":"What is the appropriate enrollment and safety decision?","options":[{"id":"risk","text":"Protect the targeted child, assess whether continued care can be made safe, and follow applicable enrollment procedures.","quality":2,"feedback":"If the harm cannot be reliably mitigated, ending care may be necessary, but the decision must be grounded in the risk and applicable procedure.","consequence":"The targeted child receives immediate protection while the director makes a documented, accountable enrollment decision.","updates":{"safety":"Protection plan active","family":"Enrollment assessed"},"critical":false},{"id":"pickup","text":"Use an alternate pickup person and assume that removes any possibility of peer mistreatment inside the center.","quality":1,"feedback":"An alternative pickup contact may reduce direct access risk but cannot by itself stop harm directed through another child.","consequence":"Direct access is reduced, but peer mistreatment may continue unless independently addressed.","updates":{"safety":"Partial controls"},"critical":false},{"id":"automatic","text":"End enrollment on the basis of the parent's previous record without documenting present risk or required program procedures.","quality":0,"feedback":"A history-based automatic response is not an individualized safety assessment.","consequence":"The program has not documented the actual risk or examined applicable procedural obligations.","updates":{"family":"Decision unsupported"},"critical":false}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The next morning","lead":"One opening employee calls out sick. The director realizes yesterday's plan had no qualified backup for routine restroom needs or simultaneous incidents.","question":"What systemic correction is needed?","options":[{"id":"coverage","text":"Verify actual supervision, activate qualified relief or restrict intake, and fund a workable backup plan.","quality":2,"feedback":"A staffing plan needs credible capacity for ordinary interruptions, not merely a compliant-looking schedule.","consequence":"A documented backup procedure is tested against predictable interruptions, with ownership and cost assigned.","updates":{"coverage":"Resilience plan adopted","safety":"Coverage verified"},"critical":false},{"id":"memo","text":"Require advance scheduling of breaks and document any exceptions to the policy for review at the next staff meeting.","quality":0,"feedback":"A reminder cannot eliminate predictable human needs or create qualified coverage.","consequence":"Staff remain without practical backup, and the same weakness persists.","updates":{"coverage":"No workable relief"},"critical":false},{"id":"volunteer","text":"Let a familiar parent volunteer supervise a small group until the regular substitute arrives and completes clearance.","quality":0,"feedback":"A helpful volunteer does not automatically qualify to supervise or count toward applicable staffing requirements.","consequence":"The plan may create a new regulatory and safety failure.","updates":{"coverage":"Unqualified substitute proposed"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{"risk":"The immediate child safety plan is in place, but yesterday's staffing problem remains.","pickup":"With direct parent contact reduced, peer safety and staffing weaknesses remain.","automatic":"The family disputes the termination while staff reveal that no qualified relief coverage exists."}}]},{"id":"hospital","title":"After the Ambulance Leaves","subtitle":"A sound first response produces a new chain of responsibilities","tag":"Medical emergency · Documentation","minutes":14,"variants":[{"id":"midday","label":"Midday emergency","start":"At 11:42 AM a child with a documented severe allergy develops breathing difficulty during lunch.","overrides":{"0":{"question":"During lunch, the allergy emergency plan is available and the child has increasing breathing difficulty. Which immediate set of actions best protects this child and the group?","optionText":{"activate":"Activate the individualized allergy action plan, administer authorized emergency medication as directed, call 911, and maintain supervision of other children.","wait":"First ask the family and physician whether the medication is needed; call 911 after receiving confirmation.","transport":"Have a teacher drive the child to a nearby urgent care office while the rest of the group is reorganized."}},"1":{"lead":"After EMS departs from the lunch area with the child and an escort, the remaining children are still at the center. Staff must manage meal cleanup, individual care plans, and a reduced qualified headcount.","question":"Which staffing and handoff checks are necessary before lunch routines resume?"}}},{"id":"outing","label":"Outdoor activity","start":"During a scheduled outdoor activity, a child with a documented allergy develops breathing difficulty.","overrides":{"0":{"question":"On an outdoor activity away from the center, a child is struggling to breathe and staff have the allergy action plan. How should the team respond while keeping the other children accounted for?","optionText":{"activate":"Follow the child's emergency plan and authorized medication instructions, call 911 with the exact field location, and assign qualified supervision for the remaining children.","wait":"Return the entire group to the center first, then ask the parent whether emergency treatment should begin.","transport":"Assign a staff driver to take the child directly to the hospital, leaving the field group to sort out adult coverage afterward."}},"1":{"lead":"EMS leaves the outdoor site with the child and one designated escort. The remaining group is away from the center, the usual adult headcount has changed, and the return route still needs supervision.","question":"What must you verify at the outdoor location before moving or dismissing the remaining group?","optionText":{"verify":"Recount children and qualified adults, establish safe coverage and a return plan, arrange replacement help if required, and name a hospital liaison.","assume":"Use the pre-outing ratio worksheet as proof of supervision until the group reaches the center, then reconcile the actual attendance.","leave":"Have the program director follow the ambulance and let the remaining adults decide how to supervise the field group."}}}}],"stages":[{"title":"An emergency begins","lead":"A child with a documented severe allergy is having difficulty breathing. Staff have the child's emergency action plan.","question":"What must happen first?","options":[{"id":"activate","text":"Follow the emergency action plan, administer authorized medication, call 911, and keep other children supervised.","quality":2,"feedback":"Urgent intervention and coverage proceed together.","consequence":"Emergency responders are called, and the remaining group has an identified qualified supervisor.","updates":{"safety":"Emergency addressed","coverage":"Roles assigned"},"critical":false},{"id":"wait","text":"Contact the parent and the child's physician for instructions, then activate emergency transport once they confirm the plan.","quality":0,"feedback":"Delaying appropriate emergency intervention can put a child in immediate danger.","consequence":"The child's symptoms worsen while the facility waits for a response.","updates":{"safety":"Emergency response delayed"},"critical":true},{"id":"transport","text":"Assign an employee to drive the child to the nearest hospital while the director arranges for the remaining children.","quality":0,"feedback":"Improvised transport may delay appropriate treatment and leaves staffing and consent issues unresolved.","consequence":"The director has introduced additional safety and staffing risks.","updates":{"safety":"Unsafe transport","coverage":"Coverage uncertain"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The transport leaves","lead":"Emergency medical services transport the child. An employee accompanies the child, reducing the number of staff on site.","question":"What must the director verify immediately?","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Count the children and qualified staff present, arrange relief if required, and name a hospital contact.","quality":2,"feedback":"Coverage is a real-time condition, not a number on the morning schedule.","consequence":"The remaining children stay supervised and a hospital liaison is identified.","updates":{"coverage":"Coverage verified"},"critical":false},{"id":"assume","text":"Keep the scheduled ratio calculation for the transport period, then reconcile physical staff presence in the daily record.","quality":0,"feedback":"Hours paid or scheduled do not establish who is actually supervising children.","consequence":"The staffing gap remains unaddressed.","updates":{"coverage":"Coverage gap ignored"},"critical":true},{"id":"leave","text":"Have the director travel with the child for continuity, with the most experienced employee covering the building afterward.","quality":0,"feedback":"The hospital needs coordination, but abandoning onsite supervision compounds the emergency.","consequence":"The center has no confirmed accountable supervisor.","updates":{"coverage":"Leadership gap"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The parent cannot come","lead":"The hospital seeks a safe discharge plan. The parent reports having no transportation and cannot arrive immediately.","question":"What is a defensible next step?","options":[{"id":"social","text":"Coordinate with the parent and hospital staff, verify consent authority, and document the plan.","quality":2,"feedback":"A transportation barrier is not automatically neglect, and the director cannot assume custody or consent authority.","consequence":"The hospital and family work toward an authorized plan while the center preserves its own responsibilities.","updates":{"family":"Discharge coordinated"},"critical":false},{"id":"custody","text":"Offer a temporary director authorization for discharge while the parent arranges transportation and the child is stabilized.","quality":0,"feedback":"A facility does not automatically acquire legal custody because of a travel barrier.","consequence":"The discharge team receives incorrect legal information.","updates":{"family":"Consent authority confused"},"critical":false},{"id":"abandon","text":"Advise the hospital that difficulty collecting a child suggests abandonment and request a child welfare assessment.","quality":0,"feedback":"Hardship alone is not proof of abandonment.","consequence":"An unsupported allegation complicates a child's discharge.","updates":{"evidence":"Unsupported allegation"},"critical":false}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The medication bottle","lead":"Staff discover a medication container within children's reach. Accounts disagree about who placed it there.","question":"What should the director do?","options":[{"id":"secure","text":"Secure the medication, preserve factual accounts, and review the intake and storage process.","quality":2,"feedback":"The responsible person must be established fairly, while the present hazard is removed immediately.","consequence":"The medication is secured and the facility begins a fact-based procedural review.","updates":{"safety":"Medication secured","evidence":"Accounts preserved"},"critical":false},{"id":"blame","text":"Record the employee who delivered the medication as responsible because that person last had confirmed possession.","quality":0,"feedback":"Arrival with medication and the later placement of it are separate events.","consequence":"A premature finding obscures the actual procedural failure.","updates":{"evidence":"Premature blame"},"critical":false},{"id":"dismiss","text":"Document a near miss, then wait for the next scheduled medication workshop to address the storage procedure.","quality":0,"feedback":"Near misses are useful evidence of unsafe conditions.","consequence":"The hazard can recur even though no injury occurred this time.","updates":{"safety":"Storage hazard ignored"},"critical":false}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The following week","lead":"The employee who accompanied the child reports burnout. The director identifies recurring coverage gaps during medical incidents.","question":"Which follow-through shows leadership?","options":[{"id":"redesign","text":"Rehearse the handoff, arrange relief coverage, inspect medication controls, and verify changed practice.","quality":2,"feedback":"Training, staffing resources, and direct observation address different parts of the event.","consequence":"A named improvement plan replaces an assumption that the incident has ended.","updates":{"coverage":"Relief plan adopted","evidence":"Practice verified"},"critical":false},{"id":"quiz","text":"Require refresher training and competency signatures before investing in coverage changes, then reassess after another incident.","quality":1,"feedback":"Knowledge refreshers can help, but do not create staffing capacity.","consequence":"Staff may know the right answer yet lack the resources to implement it.","updates":{"coverage":"Unfixed resource gap"},"critical":false},{"id":"silence","text":"Treat the response as successful because emergency services arrived, and wait for a formal complaint before making changes.","quality":0,"feedback":"Suppressing reflection prevents correction of avoidable risks.","consequence":"Employees lose confidence that safety concerns will be addressed.","updates":{"family":"Staff trust weakened"},"critical":false}],"branchLead":{}}]},{"id":"staff","title":"A Schedule That Cannot Bend","subtitle":"Build a viable staffing plan before inevitable interruptions occur","tag":"Staffing · Finance · Accountability","minutes":14,"variants":[{"id":"open","label":"Opening shift","start":"A center opens with precisely the minimum qualified coverage for the children expected that morning.","overrides":{"0":{"question":"Before morning doors open, a qualified teacher calls out and the schedule was already at minimum coverage. What should the director verify before admitting children?","optionText":{"real":"Verify which qualified staff are physically onsite, arrange an eligible replacement, and limit or delay intake unless safe coverage is established.","paper":"Leave the absent teacher listed as present for the first hour to keep the opening ratio sheet complete.","hope":"Open enrollment as scheduled and rely on a substitute arriving before a second staff member is needed."}},"1":{"lead":"At the opening shift, a qualified teacher needs an urgent restroom break while families are arriving and one child requires individualized support.","question":"How do you protect breaks and direct supervision at the same time?"}}},{"id":"close","label":"Closing shift","start":"A center begins afternoon closing procedures with no qualified relief coverage available.","overrides":{"0":{"lead":"At 4:20 PM a qualified closing teacher unexpectedly leaves early due to illness. Several children still need pickup, and there is no qualified relief staff on the premises.","question":"What must the director do before the remaining teacher handles pickups and room transitions alone?","optionText":{"real":"Recount remaining children and physically available qualified teachers, call approved replacement coverage, and restrict new or late care if safe supervision cannot be maintained.","paper":"Keep the departed closer on the time sheet until the scheduled end of shift so the building still appears fully covered.","hope":"Proceed with closing activities normally because the number of children should decline as parents arrive."}},"1":{"lead":"The remaining closer needs a restroom break while two families arrive late for pickup and a child needs one-to-one assistance.","question":"How should you plan an actual qualified relief period instead of asking staff to postpone basic needs?","optionText":{"relief":"Arrange physically present, eligible relief so the closer can take a break without leaving any child unsupervised.","no_break":"Require the closer to wait until every child is collected and shift cleanup is complete, regardless of when the break is needed.","untrained":"Ask a familiar but uncleared parent who has arrived early to watch the remaining children during the restroom break."}}}}],"stages":[{"title":"A caregiver calls out","lead":"One qualified employee calls out sick shortly before the next shift. Admissions and room allocations were planned without relief.","question":"What does the director do?","options":[{"id":"real","text":"Verify actual qualified staffing, activate an approved replacement, and limit admission if safe coverage cannot be maintained.","quality":2,"feedback":"Staffing calculations must represent people actually present.","consequence":"Intake and room arrangements are adjusted around real qualified coverage.","updates":{"coverage":"Verified staffing"},"critical":false},{"id":"paper","text":"Keep the absent employee scheduled for the first hour to preserve staffing calculations until a substitute can be found.","quality":0,"feedback":"Falsifying records hides rather than resolves a safety gap.","consequence":"The roster misrepresents supervision and exposes the facility to greater risk.","updates":{"coverage":"Falsified record","evidence":"Record integrity breached"},"critical":true},{"id":"hope","text":"Open the program as usual while contacting a substitute, since the risk of a shortage will be brief if the person arrives.","quality":0,"feedback":"Waiting for luck is not a backup plan.","consequence":"Children are admitted before adequate supervision is assured.","updates":{"coverage":"Uncovered admissions"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"A restroom break","lead":"A second employee needs the restroom while pickup duties and individualized child support are underway.","question":"How does leadership maintain coverage?","options":[{"id":"relief","text":"Use qualified relief coverage, verify supervision, and establish a sustainable break plan.","quality":2,"feedback":"Routine human needs should already be built into the staffing system.","consequence":"Qualified relief permits ordinary breaks without sacrificing child supervision.","updates":{"coverage":"Break coverage established"},"critical":false},{"id":"no_break","text":"Require staff to coordinate restroom needs before shifts and defer unplanned breaks until regular relief is available.","quality":0,"feedback":"The policy is unrealistic, potentially unlawful, and does not address the organizational weakness.","consequence":"Staff are pressured to ignore basic needs, and no backup exists.","updates":{"coverage":"No relief"},"critical":false},{"id":"untrained","text":"Ask a trusted but uncleared visitor to supervise the room briefly while staff address the immediate pickup needs.","quality":0,"feedback":"Availability does not equal qualification.","consequence":"The facility relies on a person who may not legally or safely provide coverage.","updates":{"coverage":"Unqualified coverage"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The budget objection","lead":"The owner objects to relief hours because profit margins are thin.","question":"What should the director present?","options":[{"id":"cost","text":"Compare qualified relief costs with staffing risks, attendance limits, and feasible funding options.","quality":2,"feedback":"Operational resilience belongs in budgeting, not merely emergency instructions.","consequence":"The budget now includes a documented costed coverage option.","updates":{"finance":"Coverage cost modeled"},"critical":false},{"id":"free","text":"Ask experienced staff to cover short gaps without recording extra time, then offer schedule flexibility later in the month.","quality":0,"feedback":"Unpaid labor creates wage and retention concerns and is not a reliable plan.","consequence":"Staffing instability becomes a payroll and burnout problem.","updates":{"finance":"Unfunded obligation"},"critical":false},{"id":"ignore","text":"Hire another qualified employee immediately, regardless of who has budget authority, then explain the cost afterward.","quality":1,"feedback":"Additional staffing may be necessary, but implementation needs a lawful financial and approval plan.","consequence":"The owner has no documented financing or approval path.","updates":{"finance":"Cost unplanned"},"critical":false}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The substitute arrives","lead":"An acquaintance offers to help for the afternoon but has not completed required clearance or orientation.","question":"How should the director respond?","options":[{"id":"qualified","text":"Do not count the visitor; verify eligibility and use approved coverage or adjust attendance.","quality":2,"feedback":"Staff counted for required supervision must actually meet applicable requirements.","consequence":"The facility preserves safe coverage without inventing qualifications.","updates":{"coverage":"Qualified coverage maintained"},"critical":false},{"id":"credential","text":"Count the visitor as qualified because the person's college degree demonstrates substantial classroom-related experience.","quality":0,"feedback":"Academic qualifications alone are not confirmation of required clearance or eligibility.","consequence":"An ineligible person is assigned supervisory responsibility.","updates":{"coverage":"Eligibility not verified"},"critical":true},{"id":"alone","text":"Allow the visitor to supervise independently for a short orientation period while the director completes clearances.","quality":0,"feedback":"This exposes children to an unverified and potentially unauthorized supervisor.","consequence":"Children lack verified qualified supervision.","updates":{"coverage":"Unsafe supervision"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{}},{"title":"The audit","lead":"The schedule appears compliant, but actual attendance logs show recurring uncovered intervals.","question":"What does successful evaluation require?","options":[{"id":"audit","text":"Compare real staffing with records, correct procedures, assign ownership, and verify improvement.","quality":2,"feedback":"Evidence of safer practice comes from verified operations rather than a signed policy.","consequence":"The director establishes repeated checks of real supervision and coverage.","updates":{"evidence":"Observed practice verified"},"critical":false},{"id":"sign","text":"Require all employees to sign a revised supervision policy, then close the review once attendance records are complete.","quality":1,"feedback":"Acknowledgment can be useful, but signatures do not prove adequate coverage.","consequence":"The paperwork improves while the staffing risk may persist.","updates":{"evidence":"Paper compliance only"},"critical":false},{"id":"alter","text":"Replace the inaccurate timestamps with corrected estimates so licensing staff receive a consistent-looking report.","quality":0,"feedback":"Altering records conceals rather than solves an unsafe pattern.","consequence":"The review becomes a record-integrity failure.","updates":{"evidence":"Records falsified"},"critical":true}],"branchLead":{}}]}],"evidence":[{"id":"e1","text":"An employee directly witnessed the adult shouting at the targeted child.","answer":"observed","why":"A first-person account of behavior is a direct observation."},{"id":"e2","text":"The original security recording shows the adult entering the classroom.","answer":"recorded","why":"Video establishes what is visible and audible on that recording, not everything that happened elsewhere."},{"id":"e3","text":"A colleague says the adult instructed her child to bully the other child.","answer":"alleged","why":"This is an allegation reported through another person, not a verified finding."},{"id":"e4","text":"The director expects the adult to make another complaint tomorrow.","answer":"forecast","why":"A forecast may inform a precautionary plan, but it is not an observed event."},{"id":"e5","text":"The targeted child said, 'She scared me,' and the director recorded those exact words.","answer":"observed","why":"The statement was directly heard. What it means must still be assessed in context."},{"id":"e6","text":"The video proves the mother instructed her child to retaliate, even though that exchange was not recorded.","answer":"inference","why":"This conclusion goes beyond the recording and should not be represented as evidence."}],"assessment":{"diag":[{"id":"d1","story":"A veteran lead teacher knows every classroom routine but struggles to plan payroll and relief staffing.","skill":"conceptual","cause":"training","why":"Managerial coordination can require deliberate leadership preparation."},{"id":"d2","story":"A fully trained director has only one qualified opening employee after an unexpected absence.","skill":"conceptual","cause":"resources","why":"No amount of confidence creates another qualified adult."},{"id":"d3","story":"A supervisor knows the reporting rules but publicly humiliates an employee during coaching.","skill":"human","cause":"training","why":"Interpersonal leadership and feedback are central to this concern."},{"id":"d4","story":"The building's written procedure instructs employees to backdate sign-out entries.","skill":"technical","cause":"policy","why":"A flawed policy needs correction rather than reliance on the wrong written instruction."},{"id":"d5","story":"A director notices that staff routinely initial a safety checklist without actually checking the gates.","skill":"conceptual","cause":"policy","why":"The leader must correct the verification system and test whether practice improves."}],"stages":[{"title":"A child beyond the gate","category":"Immediate safety and escalation","points":25,"lead":"During afternoon outdoor play, staff discover the side gate unlatched. A young child is found beyond the fence and appears frightened, while several children remain in the play area.","options":[{"id":"safe","text":"Bring the child to safety, check for injury, count the group, secure the gate, and arrange supervision.","quality":2,"feedback":"The first duty is immediate safety for the child and everyone remaining in care.","consequence":"The child is back under direct supervision and the gate is secured; medical and staffing questions now arise.","updates":{},"critical":false},{"id":"discipline","text":"Hold the responsible employee away from the group for immediate questioning while the other staff member checks the gate.","quality":0,"feedback":"Investigation and accountability cannot replace immediate child protection.","consequence":"The staff interview displaced immediate triage, requiring others to restore safety and arrange medical help.","updates":{},"critical":true},{"id":"resume","text":"Return the child to the group after a quick check for injuries, then investigate the unlatched gate during planning time.","quality":0,"feedback":"An elopement and unlatched gate require a substantive response.","consequence":"The incident was treated as resolved, but new symptoms and coverage risks soon emerge.","updates":{},"critical":true}]},{"title":"An emergency handoff","category":"Coverage and continuity","points":15,"lead":"The child needs medical evaluation. One employee offers to accompany the child, but coverage may fall below what is required for the remaining group.","options":[{"id":"plan","text":"Verify remaining qualified coverage, arrange relief, and appoint an authorized hospital liaison.","quality":2,"feedback":"Protect the child receiving care without abandoning everyone else.","consequence":"Qualified coverage and a liaison are in place, but a disclosure creates a separate safeguarding duty.","updates":{},"critical":false},{"id":"solo","text":"Send the most qualified adult with the child, instructing the remaining children to stay together until relief arrives.","quality":0,"feedback":"Necessary child supervision cannot be sacrificed.","consequence":"The handoff has produced a coverage gap. A disclosure now arrives through the hospital liaison.","updates":{},"critical":true},{"id":"delay","text":"Keep the child at the program for observation until staffing improves, then arrange medical evaluation if still necessary.","quality":0,"feedback":"Staffing constraints cannot justify ignoring an urgent medical need.","consequence":"Medical care was delayed. While the child waits, a disclosure introduces additional protection obligations.","updates":{},"critical":true}],"branches":{"safe":{"lead":"You have brought the child inside, secured the gate, counted the group, and observed a possible head injury. Paramedics recommend medical evaluation. A qualified employee offers to accompany the child, but doing so may leave insufficient coverage for the children who remain.","optionText":{"plan":"Confirm qualified coverage and relief arrangements before making a supervised hospital handoff; name a liaison and document the transitions.","solo":"Send the only qualified opening employee immediately and ask an untrained visitor to watch the remaining children.","delay":"Keep the child onsite until enough staff arrive, even though medical evaluation has been recommended."}},"discipline":{"lead":"You began questioning a staff member before securing the scene. A colleague now brings the child inside and reports dizziness. Another employee has left the play area, creating an immediate coverage problem while emergency evaluation is considered.","optionText":{"plan":"Stop the personnel interview, restore qualified coverage, secure medical evaluation, and appoint a trained liaison for the handoff.","solo":"Send the only available qualified employee to the hospital while the other children wait together with an unqualified office visitor.","delay":"Complete your personnel interviews before arranging the child's medical evaluation and supervision coverage."}},"resume":{"lead":"The child was returned to play without a thorough safety response. Ten minutes later the child complains of a headache. The gate is still being checked and a parent requests a medical evaluation, while staffing is already at its minimum.","optionText":{"plan":"Remove the child from play, arrange prompt medical evaluation and qualified coverage for everyone, and designate a hospital liaison.","solo":"Send the available educator with the child and temporarily leave the group supervised by a neighbor who offered to help.","delay":"Wait to see whether the headache passes, because the program cannot currently spare a qualified staff member."}}}},{"title":"A concerning disclosure","category":"Reporting and ethical boundaries","points":10,"lead":"A child spontaneously says that an employee pushed them through the gate. No video currently establishes what happened, and the employee denies it.","options":[{"id":"report","text":"Protect the child, record the disclosure, fulfill reporting duties if reasonable suspicion exists, and avoid repeated questioning.","quality":2,"feedback":"Reporting is based on reasonable suspicion rather than proof or agreement among witnesses.","consequence":"Safeguarding and reporting processes are underway, and original evidence must be protected.","updates":{},"critical":false},{"id":"prove","text":"Interview both the child and employee promptly to reconcile their accounts before deciding whether to report.","quality":0,"feedback":"Repeated questioning risks harm and can interfere with proper investigation.","consequence":"Repeated questioning may complicate the evidence trail; unaltered records are now urgent.","updates":{},"critical":true},{"id":"dismiss","text":"Note the disclosure but hold off reporting because the employee denies it and the child may be misinterpreting events.","quality":0,"feedback":"A denial or disability does not eliminate a reporting duty when reasonable suspicion exists.","consequence":"The disclosure was initially set aside, but later information requires renewed attention to protection and evidence.","updates":{},"critical":true}],"branches":{"plan":{"lead":"Qualified coverage and a hospital liaison are now arranged. During a calm follow-up, the child spontaneously says an employee pushed them through the gate. The employee denies it; no footage has yet been reviewed.","optionText":{"report":"Protect the child, record their exact spontaneous words, apply the reasonable-suspicion reporting standard, and avoid investigative questioning.","prove":"Ask the child to repeat the account in front of the employee so you can determine whose version is accurate before considering a report.","dismiss":"Treat the statement as unsubstantiated because the employee disagrees, and wait for video proof before evaluating reporting duties."}},"solo":{"lead":"The emergency escort departed before qualified replacement coverage arrived, so remaining supervision has become strained. At the hospital, the child tells the assigned liaison that an employee pushed them. The staff member disputes the allegation.","optionText":{"report":"Restore safe supervision, preserve the child's spontaneous words, and make required reports when reasonable suspicion exists without investigating the allegation yourself.","prove":"Ask the hospital liaison to interview the child repeatedly and compare the answers with the employee's account before reporting.","dismiss":"Focus on correcting the staffing lapse and defer the disclosure until corroborating evidence is found."}},"delay":{"lead":"Medical assessment was delayed while the facility waited for additional staff. While waiting, the child independently tells another educator that an employee pushed them through the gate. The accused employee denies this, and the child has not been fully evaluated.","optionText":{"report":"Arrange urgent appropriate medical attention and supervision, document the exact disclosure, and fulfill reporting duties if the reasonable-suspicion standard is met.","prove":"Delay the report until you can question the child and employee together and determine which account is true.","dismiss":"Disregard the disclosure because staff have already identified the first emergency-response mistake."}}}},{"title":"The disappearing recording","category":"Verification and documentation","points":10,"lead":"The camera overwrites old video automatically. Different employees remember different times for the gate opening.","options":[{"id":"preserve","text":"Preserve footage and original records, document conflicting accounts, and cooperate with authorized investigators.","quality":2,"feedback":"Credible records preserve what is known and what remains uncertain.","consequence":"Original evidence remains available, but the routine gate-verification system is shown to be deficient.","updates":{},"critical":false},{"id":"rewrite","text":"Create one consistent timeline based on the director's best reconstruction, replacing earlier conflicting entries.","quality":0,"feedback":"Fabricating a clean narrative destroys record integrity.","consequence":"An original note contradicts the reconstructed timeline, creating a record-integrity problem.","updates":{},"critical":true},{"id":"wait","text":"Preserve the witness accounts first and retrieve the camera footage after investigators request it.","quality":0,"feedback":"The evidence may disappear before anyone can review it.","consequence":"The recording is gone, making honest disclosure and systematic repair especially important.","updates":{},"critical":false}],"branches":{"report":{"lead":"Following the child-welfare disclosure, the required process has begun and an authorized investigator requests original records. The outdoor camera is set to overwrite the footage tonight, while staff remember different times for the gate opening.","optionText":{"preserve":"Secure the original recording and contemporaneous notes, label conflicting accounts, and supply records through authorized channels.","rewrite":"Reconcile the staff accounts into a single polished timeline and replace the original notes so investigators receive a consistent file.","wait":"Preserve staff notes but wait for a formal written video request before downloading the camera recording."}},"prove":{"lead":"Your attempt to reconcile accounts led to repeated questioning of the child and now staff disagree about what was said. Camera footage may clarify the sequence but will be automatically overwritten tonight.","optionText":{"preserve":"Stop further informal questioning, preserve the untouched footage and original notes, document the earlier interviews, and cooperate with authorized investigators.","rewrite":"Update all interview notes to one agreed timeline so later reviewers do not see conflicting versions.","wait":"Wait for investigators to ask for video before preserving it, concentrating now on a new staff interview."}},"dismiss":{"lead":"The disclosure was initially set aside. Another adult has now raised a similar concern, and the security system will overwrite potentially relevant footage tonight. Staff versions of the gate incident still conflict.","optionText":{"preserve":"Revisit immediate safeguards and any reporting duty, secure the original recording, and retain each account without converting uncertain allegations into fact.","rewrite":"Write a definitive report that identifies the accused employee as responsible so the late records are more persuasive.","wait":"Preserve only the written accounts and allow footage to expire unless someone formally requests it."}}}},{"title":"What changes next month?","category":"Consequences and equitable communication","points":10,"lead":"After immediate protections, families want answers. An audit reveals that the gate checklist was signed despite the gate repeatedly being left unsecured.","options":[{"id":"verify","text":"Share verified information appropriately, repair the gate process, observe staff, and audit its use.","quality":2,"feedback":"Safer routines require direct verification and transparent ownership.","consequence":"The response now includes operational verification and appropriately limited communication.","updates":{},"critical":false},{"id":"promise","text":"Tell families the program is safe because all staff have attended a new gate-safety refresher class.","quality":1,"feedback":"Training completion alone cannot establish operational safety.","consequence":"The promised refresher is not evidence that staff behavior or systems have changed.","updates":{},"critical":false},{"id":"blame","text":"Explain the alleged employee misconduct to families in detail to demonstrate immediate transparency and accountability.","quality":0,"feedback":"Confidentiality, fairness, and fact-based communication remain essential.","consequence":"Public statements have treated contested information as proven, risking harm to people and process.","updates":{},"critical":false}],"branches":{"preserve":{"lead":"The original video and notes have been preserved. A records review reveals that staff signed the gate checklist without checking the latch. Families request answers about what will be different next month; investigators still need confidentiality and fair processes.","optionText":{"verify":"Communicate verified information through appropriate channels, repair the gate-verification process, assign accountability, and observe whether staff actually use it.","promise":"Promise that attendance at a new gate-safety training will guarantee no similar incident happens again.","blame":"Describe the contested employee allegation to all families as established misconduct to show decisive leadership."}},"rewrite":{"lead":"You replaced conflicting notes with a single reconstructed timeline. An original staff note has now surfaced and contradicts the new version. Families ask what happened, and a gate audit shows checklist signatures without actual checks.","optionText":{"verify":"Preserve both versions, correct the record openly with authorized reviewers, distinguish facts from interpretations, and verify a new gate-check process.","promise":"Announce that a one-time refresher course fully resolves the incident without addressing the inaccurate records or checklist.","blame":"Tell families that one employee was responsible for the incident, using the reconstructed timeline without mentioning conflicting evidence."}},"wait":{"lead":"The video was overwritten before it could be secured. Families want to know why the footage is missing. A further review shows that gate checklist signatures did not correspond to physical checks, and staff disagree about the sequence.","optionText":{"verify":"Document the evidence loss accurately, communicate only verified facts, audit the checklist practice, assign qualified oversight, and test the new gate-check routine.","promise":"Reassure families that every educator completed a refresher course, so the missing recording no longer matters.","blame":"State that the accused employee probably caused the incident and share that unverified conclusion with families."}}}}]}};
